---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('brevo')!;
const b = appBySlug('aftership-email')!;
const path = '/compare/brevo-vs-aftership-email';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/brevo-vs-aftership-email');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/brevo-vs-aftership-email');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which product is being compared?",
    "answer": "AfterShip Email and SMS marketing, not AfterShip Tracking or Returns. Existing tracking use does not establish a working marketing checkout flow."
  },
  {
    "question": "What limits AfterShip cart SMS?",
    "answer": "The current guide lists Shopify, Pro or Enterprise, four destination countries, consent requirements and post-install checkout events. Confirm availability for your account."
  },
  {
    "question": "What must the Brevo import preserve?",
    "answer": "Reconcile subscription status and suppression evidence before releasing reminders; confirm the linked account first."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does the margin example prove recovery?",
    "answer": "No. It is hypothetical gross profit with fixed product cost and excludes shipping, fees, returns, messaging and staff costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Account import and checkout queue ownership.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{label:'Evaluate Brevo',tool:a.name,reason:'Confirm the Shopify account, import permissions and downstream queue.'},{label:'Evaluate AfterShip Email',tool:b.name,reason:'Account import and checkout queue ownership.'}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Brevo</th><th>AfterShip Email</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Primary check</td><td>Linked Shopify account and subscription-status reconciliation</td><td>Account import and checkout queue ownership</td></tr>
<tr><td>Budget</td><td>Quote PushOwl account scope and channel usage</td><td>Quote the required product, plan and operating support</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance</td><td>Permission, checkout entry and waiting-send exit</td><td>Checkout event eligibility and purchase exit</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Confirm the Brevo account before importing</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo’s Shopify guide</a> now uses Brevo PushOwl. Its import warning covers overwritten contact attributes, including subscription status. Export existing suppressions and compare a small set of unsubscribed, email-only, SMS-only and unconsented records before releasing messages. Installation and a later data import are different operations.</p>
<p><a href="https://docs.pushowl.com/en/articles/14033929-understanding-the-pushowl-and-brevo-relationship">PushOwl’s account guide</a> describes linked dashboards and separate PushOwl pricing. Confirm the destination if you already have a direct Brevo account; ask support about linking rather than assuming the old account is connected. Quote the actual Shopify account scope, message volume, channel credits, automation access and support. A direct Brevo starting price is not a Shopify workflow quote. Supplier documentation checked November 25, 2026.</p><h2>Compare Email against the Shopify connection</h2>
<p>Choose an AfterShip Email demonstration when checkout-triggered marketing is the job. Do not infer delivery or return suppression from ownership of AfterShip Tracking or Returns: name the event source, integration and customer/order match. Evaluate Brevo when you can reconcile the Shopify import and operate the linked account. Neither choice earns a universal winner or a third-party lifecycle recommendation.</p>
<h2>AfterShip SMS is not every cart in every country</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://support.aftership.com/en/email/articles/15440894-how-to-enable-abandoned-cart-sms">abandoned-cart SMS guide</a> lists Pro and Enterprise on Shopify, destinations US, Canada, Australia and UK, and subscribed or consenting recipients. Paid or archived orders are excluded, and events before installation do not trigger SMS. It distinguishes abandoned checkout from items merely sitting in a cart. Confirm these boundaries in the offered account; do not replay an old cart expecting a new SMS.</p>
<h2>Give the queue one owner</h2>
<p>Proposed exercise: create an eligible checkout after installation, then purchase while its reminder waits. The marketing operator checks the queue, purchase exit and event log; support handles a subscription toggle or financing question before another offer is released. Repeat with an archived order, unsupported destination and missing SMS permission. In Brevo, repeat after import and event replay to detect restored permission or duplicate entry. Keep Shopify’s own recovery and other marketing senders in the inventory; pause old entry and resolve pending sends before enabling a replacement. Preserve a rollback record without restoring already-used sends.</p>
<h2>Resolve checkout objections before offering money</h2>
<p>For international shipping shock, support supplies a current destination quote rather than a generic urgency text. Merchandising verifies size availability and removes fully unavailable baskets from recovery; a gift-message issue goes to service. For a bundle code or B2B minimum quantity, the offer owner approves stacking and quantity rules. A repeat buyer needs the correct basket link, not a fresh welcome incentive. These are operating jobs to demonstrate, not claimed vendor automation features.</p>
<h2>Two hypothetical baskets</h2>
<p>A $282 basket at 32% pre-discount margin has $191.76 product cost and $90.24 gross profit. A 9% code removes $25.38, leaving $64.86. A $216 basket at 55% margin has $97.20 product cost and $118.80 gross profit; an illustrative 11% code removes $23.76 and leaves $95.04. These percentages are examples, not recommended caps.</p><h2>Choose timing and measure the work</h2>
<p>These exercises are proposed, not performed results. Select timing, channel eligibility and contact frequency for your audience and account constraints; there is no universal email/SMS ladder. Validate local sending restrictions and each channel’s permission. Compare quotes using the same eligible audience, messages, implementation and staff workload. Include shipping subsidy, payment fees, returns, messaging and staff costs beyond the fixed-product-cost gross-profit examples above.</p>
<p>For an outcome experiment, size a randomized holdout for the traffic and detectable effect rather than selecting a fixed percentage. Reconcile orders across senders and report incremental profit with uncertainty; platform-attributed revenue alone does not prove recovery. Choose the setup that passes the relevant handoff checks at an acceptable operating cost.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
